# Assignment: Georgia Child Support 2026 Estimate Calculator
## Developer-ready spec for a statute-aligned planning tool
## Stack: Pure HTML / CSS / JavaScript, no backend for v1

## 1. Product name and positioning

Build a single-page web app called:

**Georgia Child Support 2026 Estimate Calculator**

Important product-positioning rule:

This tool is a **planning / estimate tool** that mirrors the Georgia 2026 child support calculation flow for supported cases.
It is **not** the official Georgia Child Support Commission worksheet generator and must **not** present itself as a court filing tool or as legal advice.

The app should help a user understand:
- the presumptive amount,
- the effect of parenting time,
- the effect of health insurance and work-related child care,
- optional deviation estimates for selected items,
- whether the CP or NCP would be the payer,
- each parent’s pro rata share of future uninsured healthcare expenses.

## 2. Source of truth

The implementation must be based on:
- O.C.G.A. § 19-6-15 as effective in 2026,
- the 2026 Basic Child Support Obligation Table,
- the 2026 Low-Income Adjustment Table,
- the 2026 parenting-time adjustment rules used in Schedule C,
- the official Georgia Child Support Calculator behavior for supported cases.

When the statute and a secondary explanation differ, the statute wins.

## 3. Core goal

Create a visually appealing, easy-to-use, mobile-friendly calculator that can produce a legally aligned estimate for **supported case types only**.

The app must support:
- 1 child or 2 children only,
- CP and NCP roles,
- gross income inputs,
- Schedule B adjusted-income inputs needed for supported cases,
- parenting-time adjustment under the 2026 formula,
- health insurance premium allocation,
- work-related child care allocation,
- optional dental and vision deviation estimates,
- high-income handling above $40,000 combined adjusted income,
- low-income adjustment,
- payer determination,
- detailed line-by-line result breakdown,
- save/load local scenarios,
- copy/share summary text.

## 4. Hard boundary: supported vs blocked cases

### 4.1 Supported in v1

Support only these cases:
- 1 or 2 children
- exactly 2 parents, labeled CP and NCP
- no split custody between children
- no nonparent custodian
- no SSA child benefits credit
- no VA child benefits credit
- no dependent adult child support
- no phase-in calculations
- no extraordinary-expense deviations except the specific optional dental/vision estimate described below
- no alimony deviation
- no travel deviation
- no mortgage deviation
- no life-insurance deviation
- no tax-credit deviation
- no foster-care / permanency-plan deviation
- no nonspecific deviation other than manual high-income estimate described below

### 4.2 Required blocked cases

If the user attempts any unsupported scenario, do not silently continue.
Show a blocking message such as:

> This calculator does not support this case type yet. Use the official Georgia calculator and/or a lawyer-reviewed worksheet.

The UI must block calculation when any of the following is true:
- child count is 3 or more
- split parenting / split custody is indicated
- nonparent custodian is indicated
- SSA or VA child-benefit credit applies
- dependent adult child case
- requested deviation type outside v1 support
- user says there is no court-ordered parenting time but still tries to force a parenting-time adjustment
- required income-adjustment or low-income data is missing for a supported case

## 5. Required default values

Prefill:
- Child count = 1
- CP gross monthly income = 15000
- NCP gross monthly income = 25000
- Court-ordered parenting time exists = Yes
- NCP parenting days per year = 80
- Health insurance premium = 0
- Work-related child care = 0
- Dental premium deviation amount = 0
- Vision premium deviation amount = 0
- High-income mode = Statutory Presumptive
- Manual high-income deviation amount = 0

## 6. Required inputs

### 6.1 Case setup
- Number of children: 1 or 2
- Is there a court order awarding parenting time? Yes / No
- Has the court designated the noncustodial parent? Optional helper
- Is this a split parenting case? Yes / No
- Show unsupported warning if Yes

### 6.2 Parent roles
Inputs must explicitly use:
- Custodial Parent (CP)
- Noncustodial Parent (NCP)

Add helper text:
- Georgia defines NCP as the parent with whom the child resides less than 50% of the time.
- If parenting time is equal, NCP is the parent with greater gross income, unless the court designates otherwise.

### 6.3 Gross income
For each parent:
- gross monthly income

### 6.4 Schedule B adjustments to gross income
For each parent, support these adjusted-income inputs:
- self-employment tax adjustment
- preexisting child support orders actually being paid
- qualified-children theoretical-order adjustment, if allowed

Implementation note:
These are adjustment inputs, not deviations.
They reduce gross income to adjusted gross income.

The UI should present them in an expandable "Advanced but required when applicable" section.

### 6.5 Parenting time
Do **not** use integer-only overnights as the only data type.

Required input model:
- allow **parenting days per year** as a decimal number
- permitted range for NCP parenting days: 0 to 182.5
- CP parenting days = 365 - NCP parenting days

For 2 children:
- allow either:
  1. single averaged NCP parenting-days input, or
  2. per-child NCP parenting-days inputs
- if per-child values differ, compute the average number of court-ordered days across the children

Required helper text:
- Georgia uses an annual average
- overnights usually count as days
- recurring daytime hours may require hours / 24 conversion
- this tool allows decimal parenting days for that reason

If "No court order awarding parenting time" is selected:
- disable Schedule C inputs
- do not apply parenting-time adjustment

### 6.6 Additional expenses
Support these statutory additional expenses:
- child-attributable monthly health insurance premium
- monthly work-related child care cost

For each:
- amount
- payer: CP or NCP

### 6.7 Dental / vision deviation estimates
These are not automatic presumptive support items.

Support:
- dental premium monthly amount
- dental payer: CP or NCP
- include dental as estimated deviation: Yes / No
- vision premium monthly amount
- vision payer: CP or NCP
- include vision as estimated deviation: Yes / No

Label both very clearly:
- "Court-discretionary deviation estimate"
- "Not automatically included by statute in presumptive support"

### 6.8 High-income handling
If combined adjusted gross income is greater than 40000:
- show high-income section

Support two modes:
1. **Statutory Presumptive** (default)
2. **Planning / What-if High-Income**

In Planning mode allow:
- manual monthly upward deviation amount
- label: "Court-discretionary estimate"

Do not extrapolate the BCSO table automatically.

## 7. Required calculation sequence

The app must calculate in this order and display each step:

### Step 1. Determine gross monthly income
- CP gross monthly income
- NCP gross monthly income

### Step 2. Apply Schedule B adjustments
For each parent:
- subtract self-employment tax adjustment
- subtract preexisting-order adjustment
- subtract qualified-children adjustment if entered

Outputs:
- CP adjusted gross income
- NCP adjusted gross income

### Step 3. Combined adjusted gross income
- combined adjusted gross income = CP adjusted + NCP adjusted

### Step 4. Determine / validate NCP role
Validation rules:
- if parenting time is unequal, NCP must be the less-than-50% parent
- if parenting time is equal, NCP should be the higher-income parent unless court-designated otherwise
- if equal-time data and lower-income parent is labeled NCP without court-designation override, show validation error

### Step 5. BCSO lookup
Use the official 2026 BCSO table for **1 child** and **2 children**.

Rules:
- use combined adjusted gross income
- choose the table row whose income value is **closest**
- do not interpolate
- if combined adjusted gross income exceeds 40000, set presumptive BCSO lookup income to 40000

Outputs:
- actual combined adjusted gross income
- BCSO lookup income used
- BCSO amount
- note if capped at table maximum

### Step 6. Pro rata income shares
Compute:
- CP pro rata share percentage
- NCP pro rata share percentage
- CP basic share of BCSO
- NCP basic share of BCSO

### Step 7. Parenting Time Adjustment (Schedule C)
Apply only if:
- there is a court order awarding parenting time

Inputs:
- NCP parenting days = n
- CP parenting days = c
- CP basic share = CP_share
- NCP basic share = NCP_share

Formula:
1. A = n ^ 2.5
2. B = c ^ 2.5
3. C = A * CP_share
4. D = B * NCP_share
5. E = C - D
6. F = E / (A + B)
7. NCP_adjusted_basic = NCP_share + F

Required implementation notes:
- store and display all intermediate values A through F in debug mode
- normal user mode may show only a simplified explanation
- if Schedule C is not used, NCP_adjusted_basic = NCP_share

Implementation safeguard:
To preserve the total BCSO after Schedule C, compute:
- CP_adjusted_basic = BCSO - NCP_adjusted_basic

Clamp / validation:
- do not allow NCP parenting days > 182.5
- total days must equal 365

### Step 8. Additional expenses (Schedule D)

#### 8.1 Health insurance
Rules:
- only child-attributable premium counts
- if the child-only amount is known, use it
- if coverage is for multiple persons and child-only amount is not verifiable, compute:
  - total premium / total covered persons * number of children in this case who are covered
- employer-paid premium not deducted from wages must not be counted
- enter the premium in the column of the parent who actually pays it

#### 8.2 Work-related child care
Rules:
- support monthly amount actually paid
- enter the amount in the column of the parent who actually pays it

#### 8.3 Combined additional expenses
Compute:
- combined_additional_expenses = health + work_related_child_care

#### 8.4 Pro rata shares of additional expenses
Compute:
- CP additional-expense share = combined_additional_expenses * CP pro rata %
- NCP additional-expense share = combined_additional_expenses * NCP pro rata %

#### 8.5 Presumptive obligations before deviations
Compute each parent’s presumptive subtotal:
- CP presumptive subtotal = CP_adjusted_basic + CP additional-expense share
- NCP presumptive subtotal = NCP_adjusted_basic + NCP additional-expense share

#### 8.6 Credit for actual payment of additional expenses
Apply a credit to the parent who actually pays health insurance and/or work-related child care.

Implementation method:
- for each additional-expense item, assign the full actual payment to the paying parent
- subtract that actual payment from that parent’s presumptive subtotal
- the result is each parent’s pre-deviation transfer position

This must be implemented item-by-item, not as a single vague netting step.

### Step 9. Determine presumptive transfer before deviations
Compute:
- NCP pre-deviation amount
- CP pre-deviation amount

Required output behavior:
- if NCP amount is positive, NCP is the payer
- if NCP amount is negative, CP is the payer and the payment amount is the positive equivalent

The UI must show:
- payer
- monthly transfer amount
- which parent receives the payment

### Step 10. Deviation handling (Schedule E)
Support only these deviations in v1:

#### 10.1 High-income deviation
If combined adjusted gross income > 40000 and planning mode is enabled:
- add manual monthly upward deviation amount as a separate line item
- label as court-discretionary estimate

#### 10.2 Dental deviation estimate
If enabled:
- add or subtract the deviation based on who pays it
- show it as a separate estimated deviation line
- label clearly that the court may or may not adopt it

#### 10.3 Vision deviation estimate
Same rules as dental.

Important rule:
The app is not deciding whether a deviation should be granted.
It is only estimating the numerical effect if the court adopts that deviation.

### Step 11. Low-Income Adjustment
This is required for v1.

Implement the official 2026 low-income-adjustment logic and data for supported 1-child and 2-child cases.

Requirements:
- import the official low-income table data
- apply the adjustment at the correct place in the flow, after deviations
- display when the low-income adjustment was triggered
- display the pre-adjustment amount and post-adjustment amount

If low-income logic applies and necessary data is missing, block calculation.

### Step 12. Final child support amount
After deviations and low-income adjustment:
- determine final payer
- determine final transfer amount
- display sign-safe output:
  - "NCP pays CP $X/month" or
  - "CP pays NCP $X/month"

### Step 13. Future uninsured healthcare expenses
Do not add this to the monthly transfer amount.

Compute and display only:
- CP future uninsured healthcare percentage
- NCP future uninsured healthcare percentage

Default:
- pro rata by adjusted gross income shares

Label:
- "This is not included in the monthly support amount."

## 8. Rounding rules

Use currency rounding to cents throughout the internal calculation unless exact parity testing against the official calculator shows that Georgia rounds at a different step for a particular line item.

Implementation rule:
- centralize rounding in one module
- do not scatter Math.round calls throughout the code
- expose a `roundCurrency(value)` helper

Acceptance rule:
- rounding behavior must be consistent across UI, share text, saved scenarios, and tests

## 9. Validation rules

Required validation:
- child count must be 1 or 2
- gross incomes cannot be negative
- Schedule B adjustments cannot be negative unless the field explicitly represents a credit and is named accordingly
- adjusted gross income cannot drop below 0
- NCP parenting days must be between 0 and 182.5
- CP parenting days must equal 365 - NCP parenting days
- if per-child parenting days are used for 2 children, each child input must be valid
- health, child care, dental, and vision amounts cannot be negative
- manual high-income deviation cannot be negative unless you explicitly support both upward and downward manual deviation; v1 should support upward only
- if combined adjusted gross income <= 40000, Planning High-Income mode must be disabled
- if "No court-ordered parenting time," Schedule C must be disabled

Error messages must be inline and plain-English.

